<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> The Habitat of Wildlife</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Interpret charts, graphs, and diagrams. Reading: Respond to questions based on class readings. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by applying knowledge of basic syllabification rules when decoding two-syllable or three-syllable or compound words.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can interpret a chart showing the habitats of local animals and describe key information (e.g., "This chart shows that jaguars live in Darién National Park and are considered endangered. They need large forested areas to survive.").
Can respond to questions based on readings about wildlife (e.g., "What do sloths eat?").
Can recognize common compound words and segment them accurately into syllables (e.g., "wildlife" into "wild-life").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or projects images of various Panamanian wildlife (e.g., jaguar, sloth, harpy eagle, frog, snake). Students identify the animals and briefly say where they live. "What animal is this? Where does it live?" (e.g., "It's a jaguar. It lives in the forest."). This activates prior vocabulary and knowledge of habitats introduced in Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a simple chart for specific information. For example, pointing to a mini-chart (or a simple graphic) with "Animal: Jaguar, Habitat: Forest" and saying, "This chart shows the jaguar. It lives in the forest." The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like "wildlife" (/waɪld-laɪf/) and "endangered" (/ɪn-ˈdeɪn-dʒərd/), emphasizing syllable division, and long/short vowels (e.g., long /iː/ in "eagle", short /æ/ in "bat"). The teacher explicitly demonstrates using "There is/there are" to describe a habitat: "There are many trees in this forest."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'habitat' mean what an animal eats or where it lives?" (Expected answer: "Where it lives.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to an image of a chart) "Does this chart give us information about animals or about food?" (Expected answer: "About animals.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Wildlife."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple information chart titled "Panama's Key Wildlife Habitats" displayed on a projector or as a handout. The chart lists 4-5 Panamanian animals (e.g., jaguar, sloth, harpy eagle, golden frog) and includes columns for their typical habitat (e.g., "forest," "jungle"), one key characteristic (e.g., "nocturnal," "endangered," "slow-moving"), and a simple diet note (e.g., "predator," "leaves and fruit"). The register is informative and factual. This material serves as the primary reading input for the lesson.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will read and interpret factual information presented in a chart format about Panamanian wildlife.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the chart quickly to answer: "What type of information does this chart show?" (Expected: "It shows animals and where they live.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> The teacher presents 3-4 True/False statements based on the chart. Students read the chart to determine if the statements are correct. E.g., "The sloth is a predator." (False) "Jaguars live in the jungle." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match animal names from a list to their correct habitat based on the chart.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension and interpretation of information using comparative and superlative adjectives, "there is/there are" structures, present simple verbs for habits, and target vocabulary (habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, endangered, nocturnal, protect, live, survive, diverse). Phonemic awareness: syllable division in compound nouns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Complete the Sentences":</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences that have gaps. They must complete the sentences by finding information in the provided chart. This activity focuses on reading for specific details and using the target grammar. E.g., "The ______ lives in the forest." (jaguar) "There ______ many endangered species in Panama." (are) "The harpy eagle ______ in the canopy." (lives) "The sloth moves very ______." (slowly)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Compare the Animals":</b> In pairs, students use the chart to create comparative sentences about the animals. The teacher provides sentence frames: "The [animal 1] is [comparative adjective] than the [animal 2]." and "What animal is the [superlative adjective]?" E.g., "The harpy eagle is larger than the golden frog." "What animal is the slowest?" (sloth). Students also practice segmenting compound words from the text: "Divide 'wildlife' into syllables." (wild-life).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check – "Question & Answer":</b> Students work in pairs. One student asks questions about the chart (e.g., "Where does the harpy eagle live? Is the jaguar nocturnal?") and the other student reads the chart to find the answer. They take turns asking and answering. They also ask each other: "How many syllables are in the word 'biodiversity'?" (bio-di-ver-si-ty).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Wildlife."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Panama's Animal Fact File." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a new, slightly more detailed paragraph and/or a simple diagram (e.g., a food web or a lifecycle diagram) about one specific Panamanian animal not extensively covered in the initial chart (e.g., the Tapir, the Green Sea Turtle, or another unique species). The text will include information about its habitat, diet, adaptations, and conservation status, using target vocabulary (e.g., species, adaptation, protect, conserve, resilient, diverse). Students must read the text/diagram and collaboratively create a "Fact File" by completing a template with key information:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Animal Name:</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Habitat: (e.g., "There are many forests where the Tapir lives.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Diet/Prey: (e.g., "This animal eats fruits and leaves.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Special Adaptations: (e.g., "It is camouflaged in the jungle.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Conservation Status: (e.g., "It is an endangered species.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One comparative fact: (e.g., "The Tapir is larger than the sloth.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Syllable division for a compound word from the text (e.g., "rainforest" into "rain-for-est").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly relates to "The Habitat of Wildlife" and "Panama's Wildlife" by having students research and present information on local animals, deepening their understanding of biodiversity and conservation efforts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading and interpreting, collaborative information extraction, accurate completion of the fact file template, and use of target grammar/vocabulary in writing the facts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a peer assessment of the "Fact File" during a brief gallery walk.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group posts their "Animal Fact File" (from Stage 4) on the classroom wall. Students then perform a "gallery walk," reading other groups' fact files. After reading, each student uses a simple peer assessment sheet to comment on two other groups' fact files, specifically checking for: accuracy of information from the reading, clear description of the habitat, and correct use of one comparative sentence. The teacher circulates and uses an observation checklist to note group collaboration, reading comprehension, and oral responses to questions about their fact file.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in interpreting and extracting information from the provided reading material/diagram.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to questions based on the reading (e.g., about habitat, diet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., habitat, endangered, forest, species).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of basic syllabification for a compound word (e.g., "wild-life", "rain-for-est").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and following instructions in group work.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can interpret a chart showing animal habitats and describe key information." (Circle: Yes / Mostly / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing I learned about Panama's wildlife today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about animal habitats was easy for me today? What was challenging?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying habitats help me understand why some animals are 'endangered'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully interpreted the basic chart and responded to questions. The use of "there is/there are" was generally accurate. Some students found forming comparative sentences challenging and will require more guided practice in the next lesson. The phonemic awareness activity for compound words (e.g., "wildlife") was effective and helped with decoding. The "Fact File" activity engaged students well in collaborative reading. For the next lesson, I need to provide more explicit sentence frames for comparatives during speaking practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, we will use the information and vocabulary learned from reading about Panama's wildlife and their habitats to engage in interactive speaking activities, discussing and comparing animals with partners.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>